<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kopeshvara Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–13th century</span> · Also known as Kopeshwar Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kopeshvara Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> at Khidrapur on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> river in Kolhapur district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Begun under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silahara dynasty</a> of Kolhapur in the 12th century and completed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a> in the early 13th century, it is famed for its richly carved stone, including an open pavilion supported by elephant sculptures around its base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kopeshvara-temple.jpg" alt="Kopeshvara Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shailesh.patil · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhidrapurTemple.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
